## Fleet visibility and session history (roost)

[roost](https://github.com/nickbrett1/roost) is the fleet's mission-control hub.
When `ROOST_HUB_URL` is set, `agent/roost.py` dials *out* to it and holds one
long-lived WebSocket open at `/agent/ws`; with no `ROOST_HUB_URL` the client is
not built at all and the agent behaves exactly as before. `hello` advertises
`capabilities: ["activity", "status", "sessions"]` and
`kind: "pydantic-agent"`.

It answers the `history.*` family (`history.sessions`, `history.session`,
`history.messages`, `history.search`), `sessions.list`, and `status.get`. That
history is **not a second store**: the A2A server already groups turns by
`contextId` (a session) and keeps each turn's messages in `task.history`. The
default `InMemoryTaskStore` discards that on restart, so `agent/history.py`
persists it in SQLite at `$AGENT_STATE_DIR/a2a_tasks.db` (the same writable mount
the remembered gateway id uses). A startup retention sweep keeps the newest ~200
sessions and nothing older than 30 days; `status.get.sessions.retained` reports
what is actually present.

The `sessions` token is deliberately the same one the `a2a-goose` agents
advertise, so roost's existing History UI logic covers this agent too. A method
no advertised capability promises is answered `ok: false` with an explicit
error, never an invented body, and a history-store problem is fail-open:
`tests/test_roost.py` asserts every advertised capability is actually answered,
and `tests/test_history.py` drives a real loopback WebSocket and SQLite store.

## Protocol version and wire honesty

The server is built on `a2a-sdk` — **the same SDK, and the same pinned version,
as the LiteLLM gateway's A2A client**. That is deliberate: sharing one library
version on both sides of the wire removes the client/server protocol skew by
construction, instead of relying on a shim to reconcile two dialects.

`PROTOCOL_VERSION` is pinned to `{{protocolVersion}}`. It is declared **once** in
`agent/main.py` and drives both:

- the JSON-RPC interface `protocolVersion` the served agent card advertises; and
- the dialect the endpoint actually accepts (`SUPPORTED_DIALECTS`).

The card is therefore a statement about the wire, not a wish. The LiteLLM
gateway trusts the card to choose a transport (a `0.3` interface ⇒ `message/send`;
otherwise `SendMessage`), so a card that advertises a dialect the endpoint
rejects makes every call fail before the agent runs — a green card, a green
health check, and a 500 on every turn.

`tests/test_a2a_wire.py` is the regression test for exactly that defect: it
serialises the **served** card, takes its JSONRPC interface's `protocolVersion`,
and asserts the endpoint routes that dialect's send method. If you change the
dialect — including advertising the legacy `0.3` — that test is what keeps the
card and the wire in agreement. Enabling `0.3` is a one-line change:
add `"0.3"` to `SUPPORTED_DIALECTS`; `create_app()` then turns on the SDK's
backward-compat adapter automatically.

> Why not FastA2A? The previous scaffold served FastA2A 2.0.1, whose card
> hard-codes `protocolVersion "1.0"` while its endpoint only speaks the 0.3
> dialect. FastA2A 2.0.1 is the latest release, so the mismatch is permanent for
> that library — the fix is to serve with the SDK the caller already uses.

## Reachability and ports

The agent listens on `{{listenPort}}`; the container publishes the same port
(the generator wires `docker-container` to it, and refuses a mismatch at
generation time). The card URL (`A2A_CARD_URL`) must be an address the proxy's
host can resolve — an IP or a name with working DNS, not `localhost`.

If the gateway and this container share a Docker network, prefer
`docker-container.externalNetworks` and drop the published port entirely: the
proxy can then reach the agent by container name.

## Prefer the gateway's own controls

Do not reimplement these in the agent — use them:

- **Iteration budgets**, **kill switch** and **cost tracking** per agent, at the
  gateway, enforced outside the agent where it cannot argue with them.
- **Agent permission management** (per-key/team agent access) and the **agent
  registry semantic search** (`GET /v1/agents?query=...`), which match against
  this card's name, description and skills.

`X-LiteLLM-Trace-Id` and `X-LiteLLM-Agent-Id` are forwarded on the agent's model
calls by `agent/headers.py`, so traces group and spend attributes correctly.
Without that forwarding both features silently do nothing.
